<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Serbo-Bulgarian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Serbo-Bulgarian War was fought in November 1885 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in protest against the union of Bulgaria with Eastern Rumelia. The young Bulgarian army defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> at Slivnitsa and advanced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pirot">Pirot</a> before Austro-Hungarian pressure halted it. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> of 1886 restored the prewar border, and the union was internationally accepted.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/serbo-bulgarian-war.jpg" alt="Serbo-Bulgarian War" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original uploader was ЧерноризецХрабър at bg.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S-b_war_painting_by_Antoni_Piotrowski.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>On 18 September 1885 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and the semi-autonomous Ottoman province of Eastern Rumelia declared their unification at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Plovdiv</a>. Eastern Rumelia, whose population was mostly ethnic Bulgarian, had been created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> seven years earlier. The unification went against the will of the Great Powers, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> strongly opposed it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> feared a loss of its own position in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, and its ruler, Milan I, resented the asylum that Bulgaria had given to pro-Russian opposition leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-pasic">Nikola Pašić</a> after the Timok Rebellion. Encouraged by Austro-Hungarian promises of support and territorial gains, Milan declared war on 14 November 1885, using the Bregovo dispute over a border guardhouse as a pretext.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had withdrawn its officers from the Bulgarian army when unification was declared, so the remaining officers, mostly captains, had no experience of commanding units larger than platoons, and the conflict was called the War of the Captains. Bulgaria's main forces stood on the Ottoman border, and it took five to six days to bring them west. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>'s rifles were modern, but its artillery was old, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, who took command himself, called up only the first class of infantry, about half of the available men. Bulgarian artillery, made by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krupp">Krupp</a>, was better than the Serbian guns.</p><p>Prince Alexander arrived on 16 November at Slivnitsa, where Major Guchev held a ridge with nine battalions, about 2,000 volunteers and 32 guns. From 17 to 19 November the Serbian attacks failed, with some 1,200 casualties in the first day's assault on the centre, and on 19 November Alexander ordered a counterattack. Slivnitsa was the decisive battle, and by 24 November the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> were back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. The Bulgarian army then converged on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pirot">Pirot</a>, and on 27 November, with Alexander leading the final attack, the Serbs abandoned the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 28 November the Austro-Hungarian ambassador, Count Rudolf of Khevenhüller-Metsch, visited the Bulgarian headquarters and demanded an end to the fighting, threatening that otherwise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> would face Austro-Hungarian troops. A ceasefire was signed that day. The peace was signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> on 3 March 1886 and made no change to the border. The war strengthened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>'s international position and largely preserved the unification, while it damaged the Serbian army's reputation and the trust between the two countries. Serbian historians generally blame King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>'s leadership, and the Serbian army afterwards carried out ambitious reforms.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Serbo-Bulgarian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
